<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollonia (Illyria)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apollonia was an ancient Greek city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fier">Fier</a> in Albania, founded around 600 BCE by colonists from Corinth and Corcyra. It prospered from trade with the Illyrian interior, became an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 229 BCE and stood at the western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>. The young Octavian was studying there when he learned of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s murder in 44 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollonia-illyria.jpg" alt="Apollonia (Illyria)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pudelek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apollonia,_Albania_(by_Pudelek)_-_Monument_of_Agonothetes.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
